<section
	data-testid="about"
	id="problem"
	class="py-20 lg:py-28"
>
	<div class="rounded-3xl bg-gray-50 p-8 dark:bg-gray-900 lg:p-14">
		<p class="mb-3 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-orange">01 — The Problem</p>
		<h2 class="mb-8 max-w-2xl font-monospace text-3xl text-black dark:text-white lg:text-5xl">
			Moving to a new city means eating alone — a lot.
		</h2>

		<div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-2 lg:gap-20">
			<div>
				<p class="mb-6 text-base leading-loose text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
					Communal dishes like hotpot and dim sum lose their appeal without company. Existing
					platforms like Meetup handle large events, but nothing exists for the simple, recurring
					need: finding one to three people to share a meal with.
				</p>
				<p class="text-base leading-loose text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400">
					We set out to design a mobile app that makes it effortless to find a compatible dining
					companion — not just someone who likes the same cuisine, but someone you can actually talk
					to.
				</p>
			</div>

			<div class="flex flex-col justify-center">
				<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-orange pl-6">
					<p class="mb-3 text-xl font-semibold italic leading-snug text-black dark:text-white lg:text-2xl">
						"People don't want a dining companion who likes the same food. They want someone they can
						actually talk to."
					</p>
					<cite class="text-sm font-bold not-italic uppercase tracking-widest text-orange">
						Core insight from user research
					</cite>
				</blockquote>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>
